<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 1: OVERVIEW ═══════════ -->
<section id="overview">
  <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>

  <p>This analysis draws on a definitive dataset of every Academy Award for Best Original Score, spanning 93 consecutive ceremonies from 1934 (the 6th Academy Awards) through 2026 (the 98th ceremony). The dataset normalizes the category's complex administrative history — including years when the Academy split the award into dramatic and comedy/musical subcategories — into a single canonical winner per ceremony year.</p>

  <p>The dataset captures <strong>{total_unique_composers} unique composers</strong> (counting each individual in shared-award teams separately) across <strong>{len(set(r['film'] for r in rows))} unique films</strong>. Of these composers, <strong>{repeat_count}</strong> have won more than once, producing a repeat-winner rate of <strong>{repeat_winner_rate:.0%}</strong> — meaning nearly half of all ceremonies honored a composer who had already tasted Oscar victory. This concentration at the top is one of the category's defining structural features.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Category evolution:</strong> The award originated in 1934 as "Best Scoring" and has undergone multiple name changes and rule revisions. The dataset consolidates all variants — including the separate Dramatic/Comedy scores of the 1940s–1960s and the Best Original Song Score category — into one winner per year, enabling clean longitudinal analysis.
  </div>

  <h3>Win Distribution</h3>
  <div class="chart-wrapper">
    <div class="chart-inner">
      <canvas id="winDistChart"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="text-align:center;font-size:0.9em;">The vast majority of winning composers — <strong>{win_distribution.get(1, 0)} out of {total_unique_composers}</strong> — have won exactly once, underscoring how rare repeat Oscar success is in this category.</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 2: MOST AWARDED COMPOSERS ═══════════ -->
<section id="top-composers">
  <h2>Most Awarded Composers</h2>

  <p>The Best Original Score category is defined by an elite tier of repeat winners. Three composers share the record with <strong>{max_wins} wins</strong> each — spanning dramatically different eras — while a cohort of five additional composers trail closely with <strong>3 wins</strong> apiece.</p>

  <!-- Top 3 featured cards -->
  <div class="composer-hero-grid">
    <div class="composer-hero-card">
      <div class="comp-name">John Williams</div>
      <div class="comp-wins">4</div>
      <div class="comp-era">1975–1993</div>
    </div>
    <div class="composer-hero-card">
      <div class="comp-name">John Barry</div>
      <div class="comp-wins">4</div>
      <div class="comp-era">1966–1990</div>
    </div>
    <div class="composer-hero-card">
      <div class="comp-name">Ludwig Göransson</div>
      <div class="comp-wins">4</div>
      <div class="comp-era">2018–2026</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Full Composer Rankings</h3>
  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <h4>Top Composers by Oscar Wins</h4>
      <div class="chart-inner">
        <canvas id="composerBarChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <h4>Win Count Distribution</h4>
