'''

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key insight:</strong> The '''
html += f"{best_decade}s"
html += f''' stand out as the most diverse decade with <strong>{best_decade_count} unique winning composers</strong> — '''
# Add narrative context
if best_decade == 2010:
    html += '''reflecting a fragmentation of the composing landscape and the Academy's growing willingness to recognize a wider range of musical voices.'''
elif best_decade == 2000:
    html += '''reflecting an increasingly globalized film music landscape as the Academy broadened its recognition beyond traditional Hollywood orchestral scoring.'''
elif best_decade == 1990:
    html += '''a period when the Academy recognized an unusually broad spectrum of compositional styles, from traditional orchestral work to electronic and world music influences.'''
else:
    html += '''suggesting a period of unusual openness in the category before the era of repeat-dominance took hold.'''
html += '''
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 4: ERA ANALYSIS ═══════════ -->
<section id="era-analysis">
  <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>

  <p>Dividing the 93-year history into four rough stylistic eras — Golden Age (1934–1959), Silver Age (1960–1979), Modern Era (1980–1999), and Contemporary Era (2000–2026) — reveals how the profile of Oscar-winning composers and their films has shifted over time.</p>

  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <h4>Unique Composers by Era</h4>
      <div class="chart-inner">
        <canvas id="eraComposerChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper" style="margin-bottom:0;">
      <h4>Ceremonies &amp; Composer Density</h4>
      <div class="chart-inner">
        <canvas id="eraDensityChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Era</th><th>Period</th><th>Ceremonies</th><th>Unique Composers</th><th>Defining Composer</th><th>Wins in Era</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for row in era_table_rows:
    html += f'''      <tr>
        <td class="highlight">{row['era']}</td>
        <td>{row['period']}</td>
        <td>{row['ceremonies']}</td>
        <td><strong>{row['unique']}</strong></td>
        <td>{composer_name_html(row['top_name'])}</td>
        <td>{row['top_wins']}</td>
      </tr>
'''

html += '''    </tbody>
  </table>

'''

# Generate era narrative paragraphs
for era_name in era_chart_labels:
    e = era_analysis[era_name]
    top_comp = e['top_composers'][0][0] if e['top_composers'] else 'various composers'
    top_comp_wins = e['top_composers'][0][1] if e['top_composers'] else 0
    html += f'''  <h3>{era_name} ({e['start']}–{e['end']})</h3>
  <p>Spanning {e['ceremonies']} ceremonies with <strong>{e['unique']} unique winning composers</strong>, the {era_name} was defined by {top_comp}'''
    if top_comp_wins >= 2:
        html += f', who claimed {top_comp_wins} Oscars during this period alone'
    html += '.'

    # Add era-specific characterization
    if era_name == 'Golden Age':
        html += ' This era established the template for Hollywood orchestral scoring, with the studio system funneling commissions to a relatively small circle of European-trained composers — Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Alfred Newman, and Miklós Rózsa among them. The category itself was still stabilizing, with the Academy experimenting with separate dramatic and musical score awards in several years. Won by <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and concluded with <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'
    elif era_name == 'Silver Age':
        html += ' The 1960s and 1970s saw the emergence of a new generation — John Barry, Maurice Jarre, Henry Mancini — whose work often broke from the classical Hollywood idiom, incorporating jazz, pop, and global influences. This era also marked the arrival of John Williams, whose first win in 1975 for <em>Jaws</em> heralded the return of the grand symphonic score. The era opened with <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and closed with <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'
    elif era_name == 'Modern Era':
        html += ' The 1980s and 1990s witnessed the peak of the blockbuster symphonic tradition, with John Williams and John Barry adding to their tallies. Alan Menken\'s three consecutive wins (1989–1992) for Disney animated musicals marked a historic anomaly — the only time a composer has won in three straight years. The era also saw the Academy begin to recognize more eclectic styles, from Herbie Hancock\'s jazz-inflected <em>Round Midnight</em> score to Hans Zimmer\'s breakthrough with <em>The Lion King</em>. Bookended by <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'
    elif era_name == 'Contemporary Era':
        html += ' The most diverse era in Oscar scoring history, the Contemporary period has seen an unprecedented broadening of genres and backgrounds among winners — from electronic and industrial music (Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross) to Indian classical (A.R. Rahman) and Icelandic experimental (Hildur Guðnadóttir). Ludwig Göransson\'s rapid accumulation of four wins between 2018 and 2026 has been the era\'s defining narrative, evoking the consolidation patterns last seen in the Modern Era. Opened with <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and most recently <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'

html += '''
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: NOTABLE MILESTONES ═══════════ -->
<section id="notable-milestones">
  <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

  <div class="milestone-grid">
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">First Winner — ''' + str(first_winner['year']) + '''</div>
      <div class="ms-title">''' + composer_name_html(first_winner['composers'][0]) + '''</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Won for <em>''' + first_winner['film'] + '''</em>, inaugurating the Best Original Score category at the 6th Academy Awards.</div>
