      <div class="ms-title">Hildur Guðnadóttir</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Became one of the few solo female composers to win in this category for her score to <em>{hildur_win[0]['film']}</em>.</div>
    </div>
'''

# Zmmer comeback
html += '''    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">Decades-Spanning Wins — 1994 &amp; 2021</div>
      <div class="ms-title">Hans Zimmer</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Won 27 years apart: <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) and <em>Dune</em> (2021), spanning four decades of Academy recognition.</div>
    </div>
'''

html += '''  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS ═══════════ -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>1. The Four-Win Ceiling Persists.</strong> No composer has ever exceeded four Best Original Score Oscars. John Williams, John Barry, and now Ludwig Göransson share this record — but with Göransson achieving four wins in just eight years (2018–2026), the four-win ceiling may face its most serious challenge yet.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>2. Repeat Dominance Is the Norm, Not the Exception.</strong> With a repeat-winner rate of ''' + f"{repeat_winner_rate:.0%}" + ''', nearly half of all ceremonies have gone to a previously victorious composer. This concentration is structural: composers who win once tend to be positioned for further wins through subsequent high-profile commissions.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>3. The Category's Center of Gravity Has Broadened.</strong> The Contemporary Era (2000–2026) features the most sonically diverse set of winners — spanning electronic, industrial, Indian classical, and Icelandic avant-garde alongside traditional orchestral scores. The Academy is demonstrably more open to non-traditional scoring approaches than at any prior point in the award's history.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>4. The ''' + f"{best_decade}s" + ''' Were a High-Water Mark for Diversity.</strong> With ''' + str(best_decade_count) + ''' unique winning composers, this decade produced the most dispersed winner pool on record — a sharp contrast to the concentrated dominance patterns of the Modern Era (1980s–1990s).
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>5. The Late 2020s Signal a New Chapter.</strong> Göransson's four wins since 2018, combined with first-time winners like Daniel Blumberg (2025), suggest the category is entering a period of both consolidation at the top and continued openness to new voices. The tension between these two dynamics will define the next decade of Oscar scoring.
