        html += ' This era established the template for Hollywood orchestral scoring, with the studio system funneling commissions to a relatively small circle of European-trained composers — Max Steiner, Dimitri Tiomkin, Alfred Newman, and Miklós Rózsa among them. The category itself was still stabilizing, with the Academy experimenting with separate dramatic and musical score awards in several years. Won by <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and concluded with <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'
    elif era_name == 'Silver Age':
        html += ' The 1960s and 1970s saw the emergence of a new generation — John Barry, Maurice Jarre, Henry Mancini — whose work often broke from the classical Hollywood idiom, incorporating jazz, pop, and global influences. This era also marked the arrival of John Williams, whose first win in 1975 for <em>Jaws</em> heralded the return of the grand symphonic score. The era opened with <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and closed with <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'
    elif era_name == 'Modern Era':
        html += ' The 1980s and 1990s witnessed the peak of the blockbuster symphonic tradition, with John Williams and John Barry adding to their tallies. Alan Menken\'s three consecutive wins (1989–1992) for Disney animated musicals marked a historic anomaly — the only time a composer has won in three straight years. The era also saw the Academy begin to recognize more eclectic styles, from Herbie Hancock\'s jazz-inflected <em>Round Midnight</em> score to Hans Zimmer\'s breakthrough with <em>The Lion King</em>. Bookended by <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'
    elif era_name == 'Contemporary Era':
        html += ' The most diverse era in Oscar scoring history, the Contemporary period has seen an unprecedented broadening of genres and backgrounds among winners — from electronic and industrial music (Trent Reznor &amp; Atticus Ross) to Indian classical (A.R. Rahman) and Icelandic experimental (Hildur Guðnadóttir). Ludwig Göransson\'s rapid accumulation of four wins between 2018 and 2026 has been the era\'s defining narrative, evoking the consolidation patterns last seen in the Modern Era. Opened with <em>' + e['films'][0] + '</em> and most recently <em>' + e['films'][-1] + '</em>.</p>'

html += '''
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 5: NOTABLE MILESTONES ═══════════ -->
<section id="notable-milestones">
  <h2>Notable Milestones &amp; Records</h2>

  <div class="milestone-grid">
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">First Winner — ''' + str(first_winner['year']) + '''</div>
      <div class="ms-title">''' + composer_name_html(first_winner['composers'][0]) + '''</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Won for <em>''' + first_winner['film'] + '''</em>, inaugurating the Best Original Score category at the 6th Academy Awards.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">Most Recent — ''' + str(last_winner['year']) + '''</div>
      <div class="ms-title">''' + composer_name_html(last_winner['composers'][0]) + '''</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Won for <em>''' + last_winner['film'] + '''</em> — his ''' + str(composer_win_count[last_winner['composers'][0]]) + '''th career Oscar, tying the all-time record.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">Three-Peat — 1989–1992</div>
      <div class="ms-title">Alan Menken</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">The only composer to win three consecutive years: <em>The Little Mermaid</em> (1989), <em>Beauty and the Beast</em> (1991), and <em>Aladdin</em> (1992).</div>
    </div>
'''

# Longest gap
if longest_gap:
    html += f'''    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">Longest Gap Between Wins — {longest_gap[3]} Years</div>
      <div class="ms-title">{composer_name_html(longest_gap[0])}</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">{longest_gap[1]} to {longest_gap[2]}. One of the longest intervals between Oscar scoring victories in Academy history.</div>
    </div>
'''

# Add more milestone cards
# Consecutive wins by different composers is notable too
# First female winner: Hildur Guðnadóttir (2019)
hildur_win = [w for w in individual_wins if w['composer'] == 'Hildur Guðnadóttir']
if hildur_win:
    html += f'''    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">Historic First — {hildur_win[0]['year']}</div>
      <div class="ms-title">Hildur Guðnadóttir</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Became one of the few solo female composers to win in this category for her score to <em>{hildur_win[0]['film']}</em>.</div>
    </div>
'''

# Zmmer comeback
html += '''    <div class="milestone-card">
      <div class="ms-year">Decades-Spanning Wins — 1994 &amp; 2021</div>
      <div class="ms-title">Hans Zimmer</div>
      <div class="ms-detail">Won 27 years apart: <em>The Lion King</em> (1994) and <em>Dune</em> (2021), spanning four decades of Academy recognition.</div>
    </div>
'''

html += '''  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════ SECTION 6: CONCLUSIONS ═══════════ -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>1. The Four-Win Ceiling Persists.</strong> No composer has ever exceeded four Best Original Score Oscars. John Williams, John Barry, and now Ludwig Göransson share this record — but with Göransson achieving four wins in just eight years (2018–2026), the four-win ceiling may face its most serious challenge yet.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>2. Repeat Dominance Is the Norm, Not the Exception.</strong> With a repeat-winner rate of ''' + f"{repeat_winner_rate:.0%}" + ''', nearly half of all ceremonies have gone to a previously victorious composer. This concentration is structural: composers who win once tend to be positioned for further wins through subsequent high-profile commissions.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>3. The Category's Center of Gravity Has Broadened.</strong> The Contemporary Era (2000–2026) features the most sonically diverse set of winners — spanning electronic, industrial, Indian classical, and Icelandic avant-garde alongside traditional orchestral scores. The Academy is demonstrably more open to non-traditional scoring approaches than at any prior point in the award's history.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>4. The ''' + f"{best_decade}s" + ''' Were a High-Water Mark for Diversity.</strong> With ''' + str(best_decade_count) + ''' unique winning composers, this decade produced the most dispersed winner pool on record — a sharp contrast to the concentrated dominance patterns of the Modern Era (1980s–1990s).
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>5. The Late 2020s Signal a New Chapter.</strong> Göransson's four wins since 2018, combined with first-time winners like Daniel Blumberg (2025), suggest the category is entering a period of both consolidation at the top and continued openness to new voices. The tension between these two dynamics will define the next decade of Oscar scoring.
  </div>
</section>

